WORKED. So child XML elements DO work for entity_ref. 

So the inconsistency must be in HOW I provide them. Looking at what I just did - I had `<entity_ref><kind>spec</kind><id>openapi</id></entity_ref>` which worked.

Now the issue might have been with `request` parameter - which is a complex object. Let me look at the execute spec once more:

```
{
  "name": "execute",
  "parameters": {
    "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
      "mode": {...},
      "request": {"description": "inline request content (REST: `{method, url, headers?, body?, protocol: \"rest\"}`; GraphQL: `{protocol: \"graphql\", query, variables?}`; MCP: `{protocol: \"mcp\", tool, arguments?}`. Provide `target` OR `request`, not both.", "anyOf": [{"type": "object"}, {"type": "string"}], "type": ["string", "object"]},
      "target": {"description": "EntityRef-shaped pointer to an existing `request` entity.", "anyOf": [{"type": "object"}, {"type": "string"}], "type": ["string", "object"]}
    }
  }
}
```

So `request` can be string or object. So both formats should work.

